23930:
Charged capacitor C = 2\text{ }\mu\text{F} reaches voltage V
Initial charge Q = CV and energy U_{i} = \frac{1}{2}CV^{2}
On position 2 charge is shared with uncharged 8\text{ }\mu\text{F}
Common voltage V_{c} = \frac{Q}{2 + 8} = \frac{V}{5}
Final energy U_{f} = \frac{1}{2}(10)V_{c}^{2} = \frac{U_{i}}{5} so loss 80 percent which is option C
→ (C)

23933:
Photoelectric equation gives eV = \frac{hc}{\lambda} - \frac{hc}{\lambda_{0}}
Second exposure gives \frac{eV}{4} = \frac{hc}{2\lambda} - \frac{hc}{\lambda_{0}}
Subtracting gives \frac{3eV}{4} = \frac{hc}{2\lambda} so eV = \frac{2hc}{3\lambda}
Putting in first equation gives \frac{hc}{\lambda_{0}} = \frac{hc}{3\lambda} so \lambda_{0} = 3\lambda which is option C
→ (C)

23936:
Current I = \frac{dQ}{dt} = a - 2bt which vanishes at t_{0} = \frac{a}{2b}
Heat H = \int_{0}^{t_{0}}I^{2}R\text{ }dt = R\int_{0}^{t_{0}}(a - 2bt)^{2}dt
Integration gives H = \frac{a^{3}R}{6b}
This value matches the expression which is option D
→ (D)

23937:
Potential V = -\frac{GM}{r} and acceleration g = \frac{GM}{r^{2}} with r from earth centre
Hence r = -\frac{V}{g} = \frac{5.4 \times 10^{7}}{6.0} = 9.0 \times 10^{6}\text{ m}
Height h = r - R = 9000\text{ km} - 6400\text{ km} = 2600\text{ km}
This height 2600\text{ km} is the value which is option D
→ (D)

23939:
Point in front of one slit is shifted by y = \frac{d}{2} giving path difference \Delta = \frac{yd}{D}
With d = 5\lambda and D = 10d this gives \Delta = \frac{d}{20} = \frac{\lambda}{4}
Phase \phi = \frac{2\pi\Delta}{\lambda} = \frac{\pi}{2} so I = I_{0}\cos^{2}\frac{\phi}{2} = \frac{I_{0}}{2}
This intensity value is the result which is option C
→ (C)

23941:
Escape velocity v_{e} = \sqrt{\frac{2GM}{R}} with M = \frac{4}{3}\pi R^{3}\rho gives v_{e} \propto R\sqrt{\rho}
Planet has radius 2R and density 2\rho so v_{p} = 2\sqrt{2}v_{e}
Hence \frac{v_{e}}{v_{p}} = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}}
This ratio 1 : 2\sqrt{2} is the result which is option A
→ (A)

23942:
Total flux linkage N\phi = LI for self inductance L
Given N = 1000, \phi = 4 \times 10^{-3}\text{ Wb} and I = 4\text{ A}
So L = \frac{1000 \times 4 \times 10^{-3}}{4} = 1\text{ H}
This value 1\text{ H} is the result which is option C
→ (C)

23945:
Echo from cliff acts as stationary observer for moving source
Source speed v_{s} = 15\text{ ms}^{-1} toward cliff with sound speed v = 330\text{ ms}^{-1}
Frequency striking cliff is f^{\prime} = \frac{v}{v - v_{s}} f = \frac{330}{330 - 15} \times 800\text{ Hz}
This gives f^{\prime} \approx 838\text{ Hz} and reflected echo keeps this frequency which is option B
→ (B)

23946:
Mass m = 1\text{ kg} with force \vec{F} = 2t\hat{i} + 3t^{2}\hat{j}\text{ N}
Acceleration equals force since m = 1, so integration from rest gives velocity
Velocity becomes \vec{v} = t^{2}\hat{i} + t^{3}\hat{j}\text{ ms}^{-1}
Power is P = \vec{F} \cdot \vec{v} = 2t^{3} + 3t^{5}\text{ W} which is option C
→ (C)

23947:
Full disc inertia about centre is I_{0} = \frac{1}{2}MR^{2}
Cut hole radius is r = \frac{R}{2} so its mass is m = \frac{M}{4} with centre distance d = \frac{R}{2}
Hole inertia about disc centre by parallel axes is I_{h} = \frac{1}{2}mr^{2} + md^{2} = \frac{3}{32}MR^{2}
Remaining inertia is I_{0} - I_{h} = \frac{13}{32}MR^{2} which is option A
→ (A)

23950:
Peak wavelength follows Wien law \lambda_{m} = \frac{b}{T}
Here \lambda_{m} = \frac{2.88 \times 10^{6}}{5760}\text{ nm} = 500\text{ nm}
Thus emission peaks at 500 nm so U_{2} exceeds neighbours U_{1} and U_{3}
In particular U_{2} exceeds U_{1} which is option C
→ (C)

23952:
Root mean square speed depends only on temperature as v_{\text{rms}} = \sqrt{\frac{3RT}{M}}
Pressure change does not affect it while T rises from 300\text{ K} to 400\text{ K}
Ratio gives v_{2} = 200 \times \sqrt{\frac{400}{300}}\text{ ms}^{-1} = \frac{400}{\sqrt{3}}\text{ ms}^{-1}
Value \frac{400}{\sqrt{3}} matches the stated speed which is option A
→ (A)

23955:
Objective forms real image of near object with \frac{1}{v} - \frac{1}{u} = \frac{1}{f_{o}}
With u = -200\text{ cm} and f_{o} = 40\text{ cm} this gives v = 50\text{ cm} behind objective
For relaxed viewing eyepiece takes this as object at its focus f_{e} = 4\text{ cm}
Tube length is v + f_{e} = 54.0\text{ cm} which is option C
→ (C)

23958:
Inside uniform wire B grows with radius as B = \frac{\mu_{0}Ir}{2\pi a^{2}}
At r = \frac{a}{2} this gives B = \frac{\mu_{0}I}{4\pi a}
Outside wire B = \frac{\mu_{0}I}{2\pi r} so at r = 2a this gives B^{\prime} = \frac{\mu_{0}I}{4\pi a}
Both fields equal so ratio equals 1 which is option B
→ (B)

23962:
For small angle \tan\theta \approx \sin\theta = \frac{x}{2l} = \frac{F_{e}}{mg} = \frac{1}{mg}\frac{kq^{2}}{x^{2}}
So q^{2} \propto x^{3}, giving q \propto x^{3/2}
Leak rate \frac{dq}{dt} is constant, so \frac{dq}{dt} \propto x^{1/2}v is constant
Hence v \propto x^{-1/2}, which is option B
→ (B)

23965:
\omega = \alpha t = 2.0 \times 2.0 = 4.0\text{ rad s}^{-1}
Tangential a_{t} = R\alpha = 0.5 \times 2.0 = 1.0\text{ m s}^{-2}
Centripetal a_{c} = \omega^{2}R = 16 \times 0.5 = 8.0\text{ m s}^{-2}
Net a = \sqrt{a_{t}^{2} + a_{c}^{2}} = \sqrt{65} \approx 8.0\text{ m s}^{-2}, which is option D
→ (D)

23971:
Initial kinetic energy is \frac{1}{2}mv^2 with zero potential at infinity
At closest approach this becomes \frac{(Ze)(2e)}{4\pi\epsilon_0 r_0}
Equating gives r_0 = \frac{4kZe^2}{mv^2} with k = \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0}
At fixed v this scales as r_0 \propto \frac{1}{m}
This is the option \frac{1}{m}
→ (A)

23972:
T_{cold} = 277\text{ K}, T_{hot} = 303\text{ K}, so COP = \frac{277}{303 - 277} = \frac{277}{26}
Heat removed Q_{2} = 600 \times 4.2 = 2520\text{ J s}^{-1}
Work W = \frac{Q_{2}}{COP} = \frac{2520 \times 26}{277} \approx 236.5\text{ W}, which is option B
→ (B)

23974:
At minimum deviation i = e, so i + e = A + \delta_{m} gives 45 + 45 = 60 + \delta_{m}
Hence \delta_{m} = 30^{\circ}
\mu = \frac{\sin\frac{A + \delta_{m}}{2}}{\sin\frac{A}{2}} = \frac{\sin 45^{\circ}}{\sin 30^{\circ}} = \sqrt{2}, which is option A
→ (A)

23978:
With concentrated acid copper is oxidised to Cu(NO_{3})_{2}
Concentrated HNO_{3} is reduced to NO_{2}
Balanced equation gives Cu(NO_{3})_{2} and NO_{2}, which is option D
